@book{Valentin2012,
abstract = {In February 2000, scientists announced the draft completion of the human genome. If media reports were accepted at face value, then it might be reasonable to predict that most geneticists would be unemployed within a decade of this announcement and human disease would become a distant memory. As we all know this is very far from the truth, the human genome is many things but it is not in itself a panacea for all human ailments, nor is it a revelation akin to the elucidation of the DNA double helix or the theory of evolution. The human genome is simply a resource borne out of technical prowess, perhaps with a little human inspiration. One thing that is certain is that we do not yet understand the functional significance of the majority of our genome, but what we do know is finally put into context.},
